Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Five Syllables Borrow the Fourth Beat
- Story & Context: During a rooftop rehearsal, Meera writes a five-syllable phrase across a standard four-beat bar and asks the dancers not to count it, only to keep returning to Kabelo’s clap. The first several cycles feel stable, but each repetition places the vocal accents against a different part of the electronic pattern. Kabelo keeps the kick unchanged and allows only two synth-bass notes per bar, making the shifting syllable alignment the main event. Arash answers the phrase with five santur attacks spread across two pitches, while Nisha inserts one short bass-recorder tongue stop after the final syllable.

Halfway through, Meera compresses the same five-syllable phrase into half the duration without changing its order. The drum machine does not speed up, yet the track suddenly feels as if it has doubled its internal velocity. The final section returns to the original spacing, but the dancers’ claps now land with greater confidence because the displaced accents have become familiar.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Chalk marks showing five short syllable strokes stretched across four large beat boxes on a rooftop floor, microphone cable crossing the grid, santur mallets beside two marked strings, bass-recorder keys catching dusk light, small drum machine on a classroom desk. Vocal material should consist of original non-lexical konnakol-style rhythmic syllables and count cues only; recurring spoken English fragments limited to “five across four,” “keep the clap,” “same order,” and “back to one.”

Track 2: Santur Answer After the Red Chalk Circle
- Story & Context: Arash notices a faded red chalk circle left from a school sports drill and proposes a performance rule: he may answer Meera only when her phrase ends while she is physically standing inside the circle. Meera begins pacing around the rooftop as she performs alternating 7- and 3-syllable cells. Kabelo maintains a bright Shangaan electro groove with minimal bass, forcing the ensemble to rely on handclaps and keyboard stabs for lift.

Each time Meera steps into the circle at a phrase ending, Arash responds with a santur dyad whose second note is damped immediately by hand. Nisha shadows the first dyad of every fourth response with a breath-heavy low note. As the track develops, Meera deliberately delays entering the circle, creating long passages with no santur at all. The absence becomes more noticeable than the instrument itself.

In the final minute, she remains inside the circle and performs rapid alternating cells. Arash answers every phrase until the texture becomes a dense conversation, then both stop together on a single group clap.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Faded red chalk circle on a school rooftop with a vocalist stepping into it beside a close microphone, santur positioned just outside the line, bass recorder leaning against a plastic chair, compact drum machine and small speaker behind them. Vocal material should be rhythmic syllables rather than semantic verses; short spoken cues limited to “inside now,” “answer here,” “leave it empty,” and “one clap.”

Track 3: Rain Starts on the Seventh Return
- Story & Context: The closing track is built from a rehearsal interrupted by the first light monsoon rain. Meera establishes a seven-cycle konnakol cadence, and the ensemble agrees that no major arrangement change can happen until the cadence has returned seven times. Kabelo begins with only kick, clap, and one bright keyboard note. Arash uses dry damped santur strikes, while Nisha performs key clicks without pitch.

On the seventh return, the first raindrops begin hitting the rooftop water tank and concrete. Rather than sampling them into a decorative ambience layer, Kabelo leaves the live microphones open and lets the rain gradually obscure the smallest handclap details. Meera slows the density of her syllables but not the underlying tempo, creating wider pockets between phrases. Arash removes the felt dampers so the santur can ring longer into the wet air, and Nisha finally introduces fully pitched bass-recorder notes.

The electronic percussion is progressively stripped away until only the recurring clap landmark remains. The EP ends with Meera completing the seven-cycle cadence one last time while rain noise has become louder than the drum machine.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: First monsoon drops darkening chalk rhythm marks on a concrete school rooftop, stainless water tank covered in small rain impacts, santur with felt dampers removed, bass recorder in the player’s hands, vocalist continuing rhythmic hand gestures under light rain. Use non-lexical rhythmic syllables and sparse spoken cues only; recurring fragments limited to “seven returns,” “do not change yet,” “let it ring,” and “finish in the rain.”

